# Intent: Fixes for the pairing exchange. (1) The unit refuses an all-zero or small-order X25519 host key (the eight known points, with the top bit set or not) and an all-zero shared secret, so a link key never depends only on public values; the reason is bad key. (2) A "paired" message on the plain link no longer gives the unit a key: the key is derived but held, and the unit asks the owner to tap Keep on its own screen ("Keep this host? Tap Keep only if you just typed NNNNNN on the host yourself"); Cancel, or a minute with no tap, wipes the key and sends pair_abort to the host. A "paired" message that did not come from the owner's own typing is therefore not kept unless the owner taps Keep. Plain-link pairing still assumes nobody is interfering with the link during the owner's few seconds at the host; docs/plan/link-key-exchange.md says so. Tests: the pairing core test (vectors for the eight points, the top-bit forms, the zero secret, the confirm, the cancel and the timeout).
# Placement: unit
# Upstream: firmware/main/hal/maipai_pairx_core.h and maipai_linkpair.cc (ours)
# Check after a flash: on a linkpsk image pair with a host: after typing the six digits on the host, the unit shows Keep this host? with Keep and Cancel; Keep stores the key and shows Paired; Cancel (or a minute with no tap) leaves the unit unpaired and the host is told.
# Kconfig: none (compiled with MAIPAI_LINK_PSK)
# Memory cost: 36 bytes in the pairing state while it runs; the confirm screen is 7 LVGL objects (about 1 KB internal) for a few seconds.

